//! upon zero divisors and imaginary quantities simultaneously. The very origins of
//! the algebraic theory of equations demanded imaginaries as intermediary terms,
//! as Cardano found when he discovered the general form of the cubic equation in
//! the Renaissance. And as is well known, the intractibility of quintic equations to
//! extensions of such methods led to the classic group-theory-spawning studies of
